q45|n|To free from accusation, or the imputation of fault or blame; to clear from guilt; to release from a charge; to justify by extenuating a fault; to exculpate; to absolve; to acquit. ; To pardon, as a fault; to forgive entirely, or to admit to be little censurable, and to overlook; as, we excuse irregular conduct, when extraordinary circumstances appear to justify it. ; To regard with indulgence; to view leniently or to overlook; to pardon. ; To free from an impending obligation or duty; hence, to disengage; to dispense with; to release by favor; also, to remit by favor; not to exact; as, to excuse a forfeiture. ; To relieve of an imputation by apology or defense; to make apology for as not seriously evil; to ask pardon or indulgence for. ; The act of excusing, apologizing, exculpating, pardoning, releasing, and the like; acquittal; release; absolution; justification; extenuation. ; That which is offered as a reason for being excused; a plea offered in extenuation of a fault or irregular deportment; apology; as, an excuse for neglect of duty; excuses for delay of payment. ; That which excuses; that which extenuates or justifies a fault.	a:s8a,93,6;e7h,53,2;1hkp,31,4;1oc3,23,3;vrg,14,5;1oab,14,5;1kbf,9,1;16i4,4,4~s8a,93,6;vrg,14,5;1oab,14,5;16i4,4,4;1hkp,31,4;1oc3,23,3;e7h,53,2;1kbf,9,1	n:e7h,43,1;1dte,16,1~e7h,43,1;1dte,16,1
